<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n42422" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">sălūtātrix</span></b> ,
			īcis, f.
		salutator (postAug.), <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>she that salutes;</i> occurring only in
			apposition. </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b> In gen.: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">pica,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Mart.
					7, 87, 6</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">charta,</span>”
				i. e. <i>a letter of greeting</i>,
				 <i><span class="en">id.
					9, 99, 2</span></i>
			.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b> In
			partic., <i>she that makes complimentary visits</i>,
				<i>that pays court</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">turba,</span>”
				i. e. <i>the crowd of clients who come to salute their
						patron in the morning</i>,
				 <i><span class="en">Juv. 5,
					21</span></i>
			.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
